 procedures were recorded  but here one of the things that we're  trying currently is the google glass so  if you're not familiar with google glass  um it's  something that you wear just like  glasses and on the side here it  it has a camera right here so you can  very easily record and then you can  provide feedback or the student can  visualize also how they're doing  and improve on their technique as well  so again  to me  it's really  challenging  and shocking  that we do provide  all of this training for  biomethodologies but when it comes to  surgical training we do not so obviously  the question comes up why is that  um  why aren't we doing that  and i think we know the answers to that  one it's money it's expensive to have  these tools  and  also the personnel having personnel that  is experienced in various surgical  procedures and rodents there's not going  to be one person that knows all of the  procedures that are being performed  so it's really challenging to identify  folks who have the surgical background  and have been taught and know how to  perform these procedures correctly and  then all of that includes time the  gastric bypass procedure you can't learn  that within a week even within a  two-week span it becomes challenging for  folks to become competent in that  procedure but we all want to squeeze  that into you know learning in one day  or two days it's just does it really  does not work  and also when we're looking at with the  presentations they were here earlier  today  you know with this with the new guide uh  the european uh regulations they have  now been implemented  none of those  re  guide guidelines and also even the law  here animal welfare  law in the united states they do not  allow us to justify for persona not to  be well trained because of finances  or lack of time  so  if we want to get away from  all of these publications are coming out  that animal studies are predictors we  really have to improve the way the  techniques are done  and maybe that not every phd student is  going to be able to do these procedures  you know either you can outsource  there's a lot of  organizations companies that provide  surgical models um and they have the  experience  in those models someone performing a  procedure you know for two weeks then  doing the studies for a year and then  performing again in two weeks just does  not work so unless we can implement  those things  i think that we're still going to  continue to see these type of  publications  and we also have to remember that  performing animal research is a is  really a privilege  and  we really have to try to fix things  internally  so make these refinements implement this  training  because if we don't it's going to  be  pushed upon us externally from  organizations that are not going to  provide us with as much freedom as we  have right now  and that's going to be really really  challenging that potentially we could  lose  the capability of performing animal  research or being significant or  restricted here in united states um as  you know the presentations they were  presented early this morning we know  that in uk there's significantly more  restrictions there as also in in europe  and in european union  so again if we want to change that we  should try to do that internally before  it's being forced upon us from external  forces  and this is something that um i wish i  remember who said this but it is easier  to defend an idea  than it is to implement it and this is  certainly true with training we all talk  about training every conference that we  go to it talks about training and now it  also talks about competency assessment  but we really have to start implementing  it  and you know simple things like  the the grants that we receive from the  from nih  none of those grants applications  actually talk about having money for  training um there's some for conferences  for example going i think it's like a  thousand dollar 15 1500 something like  that it's very limited um so we really  have to change our whole field  and how we think about training and how  we can incorporate the training  so  the main message from this presentation  i hope uh that was able to convince you  is that having appropriate training and  assessment  allows us to perform procedures  correctly which allows us to standardize  and reproduce our research which in the  end is going to increase  the ability to translate studies from  pre-clinical studies to clinics and  overall it's also going to improve the  animal welfare  and follow with the three r's of  reducing the animals use um  as well again  improving the animal welfare  so at this point  i just again one would like to thank you  for attending this  presentation and i'll be more than happy  to take i think we have some time  to take questions um if there are any  so one of the questions um what tools  actually make sure i have all of them  what tools do you use  for your phase 2 training with  non-animal models and more complic  complicated types of surgery  there really doesn't seem to be great  models for certain types of surgeries  sister  magnet cannulations microdialysis also  do you have trainees practice and  euthanize animals which have been  euthanized for another reason other than  training before moving on to live  animals  so the first part it's definitely true  we do not have a lot of good models  that are  available to us for these more  complicated procedures  but i think starting with the basics  is a good idea but then also that's why  one of the reasons why we  try to develop the model that i just  showed you here and the capabilities to  develop these models is there um i mean  so for example that  organization we work here at wake forest  they have a human model um that pretty  much you know when you make an incision  the skin bleeds um the muscles tear and  if you  introduce some kind of drug into that  person the heart rate goes up for the  respiratory you know all those different  things it's feasible it's there  but we're not willing to pay for that  that's one of the huge challenges that  we have to rethink and we have to  provide funding  for this type of training and these  models are  it's feasible to develop these models  but we need to provide the finances to  be able to purchase and utilize those  models  but in the meantime we can use some of  the models from the human surgery side  and incorporate them into  into the into the lab animal side and  there's various catheterization models  that we utilize for example uh for uh  from the human surgery side  and i can also  send you some more information about  this as well as regard to euthanized  animals we definitely do utilize those  implement them  and that's when we talk about utilizing  animals for training  and that  portions of that  um i guess when i talk about animals  talk about euthanized as well as  recoverable animals  and it's something that's useful  uh but it's also  the tissues feel very differently  obviously the tissues don't bleed so  that's a significant challenge but it's  also for some of the procedures it's a  it's a good step forward for the  gastrointestinal anastomosis for example  i don't think it is the tissues  deteriorate very quickly so they handle  very very differently  than live tissues so for the gastric  bypass procedures we have for training  we actually move from inanimate models  straight to  live animals  so next question how do you assess  proficiency  with specific procedure if you don't  have access to experienced people to  assess  the trainee's ability  i mean that's an excellent question  and this is where again  for us we have developed various tools  various assessment forms for all of the  procedures that we teach  and they're very similar so we have  two  assessment tools we have a global tool  and again this is translated from the  human surgery side that sense is kind of  the general uh skills that you would go  from procedure to procedure like for  example making knots or suture patterns  and then we have the specific  assessment tools for each procedure  describe step by step and the way i  showed you that example there  but again  if you don't have the person you can't  assess that person correctly  so it's for a lot of institutions i know  that's a huge huge challenge uh because  for example if there's someone you know  wants to do a gastric bypass procedure  and there's no one at that institution  that has done that um or has the  background in that you're not going to  be able to assess those folks  so  that's something that again if we ended  up  as a laboratory animal community pulling  a consortium  that  would develop  these type of tools together um then we  would be able to share them through our  community and really allow us to  improve the way we do research  but at this point that is challenging  but if you don't have someone with that  background  i mean unfortunately you can't do that  type of assessment  so i'm looking for this i don't see any  other  questions and make sure i miss anybody  so again i would like to thank you for  attending  uh this presentation and also i want to  mention um that if you could go and  visit the vendor hall  because again this conference would not  be feasible without the sponsors  so we would really appreciate if you  could do that  oh and there's a couple more questions  just came in um so one i am interested  in your thoughts on performing  assessments during surgery on approved  research protocols  obviously this has complications for  introducing variability to research  related to surgical proficiency but  reduces animal use for training  just make sure i understand the question  i'm interested in your thoughts on  performing assessments during surgery  so  obviously there are some like i believe  in uk  you can't use animals for training the  animals that you perform surgical  procedures on are already on the studies  and that's a significant challenge i  don't think you can really  do that so give an example you know if  you were going into a hospital for a  gastric bypass  and your options were  in resident who has never performed this  procedure is going to perform a  procedure while someone is assessing  them on it or you get an expert  performing a procedure obviously at  least i hope most of you guys if not  everybody would pick the expert surgeon  so again the person being there if  they're not trained doing those  assessments um in that type of  um  in that in their day in the  studies  to uh to me it doesn't really make sense  because you're going to introduce a lot  of different variables in there and i  understand the whole idea of looking at  the number of animals that you utilize  but i think it's been well demonstrated  even with their studies that you have  shown that the poorly done studies  really do not lead to good results or  translation into clinics and we have  spent enormous amount of money and  enormous amount of animals that we have  used  during those times so again  if we can utilize  the animals initially for training and  assessment  overall it's going to significantly  decrease the animals that number of  animals that we utilize  because again you decrease the variables  you decrease the number of animals that  you need for study to get the right data  and statistics  so where do you get the inanimate models  and so there's different  companies that provide them and i can uh  provide that information  uh to the folks that are attending this  specific presentation we can email them  to you after the conference uh but the  mouse model that i demonstrated that's  something that  um  we have to develop internally because  there's just nothing  like that out there  so i don't see any more questions so  again thank you so much and i hope you  guys are enjoying the conference um and  enjoy the rest of the day with the  presentations and also 